  "payload": {
    "role": "assistant",
    "type": "assistant_message",
    "content": "Testing complete — all 26 checklist items executed and recorded.\n\n**Results: 21 PASS / 5 FAIL**\n\nThe core experience is solid: browsing, template selection, form filling, Markdown and plain-text preview, copy-to-clipboard, favorites (including persistence), filtering/sorting, live suggestions, and reactive result counts all behaved as expected.\n\nThe 5 failures cluster around **input validation**, which is the weakest area of the app:\n\n- **FT-10** — Search is literal substring matching only. \"resme\" and \"meting\" both returned zero results and zero suggestions, so the promised fuzzy/typo-tolerant matching is absent.\n- **CS-14** — Format-typed fields aren't validated. An `email` field accepted `not-an-email` and a date field accepted `99/99/notadate`, both flowing straight into the generated output with preview still enabled.\n- **CS-17** — Only `< > { } [ ] \\ | \\`` are blocked. Markdown-structural characters pass through, so summary text containing `# INJECTED H1` and `* item` rendered as a genuine `<h1>` and `<ul>` inside the resume — real formatting injection.\n- **CS-27** — No placeholder/field consistency check. A template with 4 placeholders but 1 declared field submitted cleanly, and its preview rendered three literal `{{...}}` placeholders.\n- **CS-29** — Duplicate field labels are neither rejected nor disambiguated. Two fields labelled \"operator\" produced two inputs sharing one id and one state value: typing in one mirrored into the other, and it triggered React duplicate-key console errors.\n\nWorth noting that CS-16 and CS-19 pass, so the app *does* implement required-field gating well — the gap is specifically in validating the *content* of values rather than their presence."
  }
}
